Implementation approach: scan with a combined regex alternation, escape non-match segments, emit tags for matches. Regex:
`(!\[([^\]]*)\]\(([^)]+)\))|(\[([^\]]+)\]\(([^)]+)\))|(`([^`]+)`)|(``?)...`

Order matters. I'll write a scanner:

```dart
final _inline = RegExp(
  r'!\[([^\]]*)\]\(([^)\s]+)(?:\s+"([^"]*)")?\)'   // image
  r'|\[([^\]]+)\]\(([^)\s]+)(?:\s+"([^"]*)")?\)'    // link
  r'|`([^`]+)`'                                      // code
  r'|\*\*([^*]+)\*\*'                                // bold
  r'|(?<!\*)\*([^*\n]+)\*(?!\*)'                     // em — dart regex supports lookbehind? Dart RegExp is JS-like: NO lookbehind!
```

Dart RegExp: no lookbehind support (lookAHEAD is supported). So em matching without lookbehind: `\*([^*\n]+)\*` and ensure bold processed first (regex alternation order: bold tried at each position before em, so `**x**` matches bold at position). But overlapping: for `**bold**`, at pos of first `*`, alternation tries bold first → matches `**bold**`. Good. For `a *b* c`: at `*` (single), bold fails, em matches `*b*`. Edge `*a **b** c*` — rare; acceptable.

Also `_em_`? The post uses `_` in text like `>_Fa`... risky. Support `_em_`? `>_Fa` has `_Fa` — `_em_` regex `\_([^_\n]+)\_` would match `_Fa blog — building..._`? Only if two underscores on a line with text between. In the blog post there are lines with multiple `_`? "the harness that builds itself." no underscores. But ">_Fa blog" title line has one `_`. Tables have `~$0.15 / 1M` no underscore. Safer: skip `_em_` support (rare in these docs). Docs md may use `_em_`? Possible but acceptable loss. Actually docs/tool-availability etc. may use `snake_case` with underscores everywhere — definitely skip underscore-em.

Strikethrough `~~`: cheap to add: `~~([^~]+)~~` → <del>.

Autolinks `<https://...>`: add `<(https?://[^>]+)>` → <a href>. Cheap.

Tables: parse row by splitting on `|` respecting no escapes (fine). Alignment from delimiter colons → style/text alignment attr. Output:

```
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Token type</th>...</tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>...</td>...</tr>...</tbody>
</table>
